<title>FRANCE: Maastricht must be interpreted strictly - De Silguy.</title>
<headline>Maastricht must be interpreted strictly - De Silguy.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-01-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>There must be no softening in the Maastricht Treaty criteria for entry into Europe's single currency, European commissioner Yves-Thibault de Silguy said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;The euro must be a good currency,&quot; de Silguy told a bankers' conference. &quot;There is no question of softening the criteria.&quot;</p>
<p>Asked about the relationship between the euro, due to be launched on January 1, 1999, and the dollar, he said it was up to financial markets to determine the correct parity.</p>
<p>&quot;Our interest is to have stable currency,&quot; de Silguy said.</p>
<p>He said Europe, when deciding which countries qualified to join a single currency at the start, must take into account budgets planned for 1998.</p>
<p>Heads of state and governments will decide in early 1998 on the basis of fiscal and monetary performance in 1997 which countries will qualify for a single European currency.</p>
<p>(( -- Paris Newsroom +331 4221 5452))</p>
